text:
alex: ran a couple nsys + perfetto traces on the nightly runner, and noticed some weird kernel-selection flips during batched requests. attaching the capture link in the thread below, but summary: large prefix hits in KV cache cause selector to pick an optimized 'short-seq' kernel, then later tokens fall back to the long-seq path -> big stalls. :confused:
maria: ugh. did you correlate with NVTX ranges? we need solid span boundaries to map selector decisions to request phases.
alex: yep — perfetto shows NVTX ranges overlapping with batching boundaries right where the selector decision happens. but ranges are named generically ("runtime:op") so it's hard to tell which request and which stage.
jason: can you paste the nsys command you used? also curious about sampling vs continuous traces.
alex: ran: `nsys profile --output nightly_run --trace=nvtx,cuda,osrt --force true --sample=none --duration=30s` then imported into perfetto.
ravi: two things: 1) naming matters (we should include request id + stage), 2) watch NVTX call overhead. pushing an NVTX for every tiny span can inflate overhead and change scheduling. :warning:
linh: agree with ravi. for kv-cache we already have per-token hooks. If we add per-request NVTX that wraps many tokens we can keep it coarse, then add fine-grain only for problematic kernels.
maria: proposal: adopt a hybrid naming scheme: `R:<short-req-id>|S:<stage>|K:<kernel-hint>` e.g. `R:7a9|S:kv-prefetch|K:short-seq`. short-req-id (6 chars) to keep labels small in nvtx.
jason: that encoding is compact, good idea. also add a numeric span-type tag so tooling can colorize (0 = coarse, 1 = fine).
alex: example C++ helper I used:```cpp
nvtxRangeId_t pushRequestSpan(const char* tag) {
  nvtxEventAttributes_t attr = {};
  attr.version = NVTX_VERSION;
  attr.size = NVTX_EVENT_ATTRIB_STRUCT_SIZE;
  attr.messageType = NVTX_MESSAGE_TYPE_ASCII;
  attr.message.ascii = tag;
  return nvtxRangeStartEx(&attr);
}
// pop with nvtxRangeEnd(rangeId);
```
ravi: for python/runtime side, keep the string construction cheap: `f"R:{req[:6]}|S:{stage[0:12]}"`. avoid dynamic allocations in hotpath.
linh: note: kernel selector currently reads prefix_len and batch_size. If a cached prefix inflates prefix_len early in the request, the selector picks kernels optimized for that distribution; we should add a stage flag that indicates "KV-hit prefix" so selector can ignore prefix_len for first decision.
maria: can we instead make selector consult a per-request hint field that instrumentation sets? that avoids overloading NVTX for logic. NVTX stays purely for tracing.
alex: good call. we can have `request.metadata.selector_hint = "kv_prefetch"` set at prefetch time. selector checks if hint exists and will bias decision.
jason: test plan: 1) change NVTX naming as proposed, 2) add selector_hint field and short-circuit decision path, 3) run same nsys job and diff timeline. keep batch heuristics unchanged for first pass.
ravi: I'll own the instrumentation changes (NVTX naming + helper). alex can add selector_hint wiring, jason runs the perf jobs. linh will validate that KV cache hook sets hint only when safe.
perf-bot: [regression-alert] nightly-run: kernel switch diff +18% tail latency between 2315552000 and 2315559000 - investigate.
alex: linking the perfetto share: https://perfetto.dev/trace/abcd1234 (contains span colors and kernel tracks)
maria: one more guideline: cap NVTX message length at 48 chars, include no PII, and avoid per-token unique ids. stick to 6-char request hash only.
jason: also add a short lint in CI that flags NVTX calls in hot loops unless annotated as fine-grain-exempt.
alex: ok let's do the experiment tomorrow morning. I'll post updated snippets and a small benchmark (1000 batched requests) when ready. :thumbsup:
